WEBVTT

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:42.060
The Natural History Museum, one of the most popular of all London's attractions.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:51.030
Sometimes it gets so crowded that it can be quite difficult to see the exhibits as closely as you might wish.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.700
Ladies and gentlemen, the museum is going to be closing in five minutes, so please make your way towards the exits.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.180
Thank you.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.940
So it's a great treat if, somehow or other,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:10.550
you can manage to look around when all the other visitors have gone.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:30.320
Thank you.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.740
creatures here might

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.910
if you were lucky, have seen in the wild.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:40.180
But there are certain ancient animals

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.340
that we'll never see with our own eyes.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.220
Extinct.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:59.820
Among them, one or two mysterious, not to say suspicious,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:21.800
characters that i would like to examine as they were when they were alive it's a big place there

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:29.880
are 70 million or so specimens here i'm told and the first i want to look at right now is way up

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.230
on the very top floor.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.620
This, some might say, is the most scientifically important

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.740
and valuable specimen in the whole of the museum.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.120
It's a fossil called Archaeopteryx,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.330
and it was secured from the museum

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.730
by the first director, Professor Richard Owen,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.230
back in 1862.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:57.610
Getting it wasn't easy.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.820
There was a lot of international competition.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.480
and there was a certain amount of skullduggery

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.760
and it certainly cost a small fortune.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.290
But what kind of creature was Archaeopteryx when it was alive?

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.040
It had two long leg bones, so it must have stood upright.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.600
Bony tail and a long neck.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:36.560
Its head had bony jaws packed with teeth, like a reptile's,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:41.610
and its arms had three elongated fingers, each ending with a claw.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:49.800
So you might think it was some kind of strange, spindly-armed, upright-standing lizard,

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.650
except for one fact.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.970
where it's evidence of more than just bones on its slab, feathers.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.830
Archaeopteryx lived some 150 million years ago,

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.670
long before the appearance of true birds.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.280
Those feathers on its arms certainly enabled it to glide.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:47.200
But that's not all. It had powered flight.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.040
Marks on the bones show that there were enough muscles attached to them

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.060
to enable it to flap.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:05:59.980
Not only that...

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.440
A recent scan of its skull showed that its brain

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.740
would have given it the senses and reactions

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.220
that are needed for accurate control in the air.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.460
This creature was half reptile, half bird.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.790
It was the first proof that in prehistory,

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.460
there were intermediate forms that link the big, very different groups of animals

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.870
that we know today.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.180
But while Archaeopteryx could certainly fly,

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.880
it could also clamber up tree trunks and along the branches

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.000
like a tree-living reptile, thanks to those clawed fingers.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:59.970
There were insects flying around at that time, and Archaeopteryx...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.800
Patrix's teeth show that it was a hunter.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.090
This is Professor Richard Owen,

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.980
the man who acquired that fossil and built this museum.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.880
Although he disagreed with Darwin's views on evolution,

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.740
he was one of the great scientists of his time,

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:39.810
and he had a particular flair for interpreting fossils.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:48.760
In 1839, a huge thigh bone was sent to the museum from New Zealand.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.520
Owen deduced from its internal structure

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.630
that it must have belonged to a bird.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.600
If so, it must have been a giant.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:03.430
Stories of New Zealand had stories of giant flightless birds

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.070
that had once roamed their islands,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.200
but Europeans had dismissed them as myths.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:14.220
But eventually, Professor Owen acquired enough bones of these huge birds

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.670
to put together a complete skeleton of one of them.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.480
This was no myth.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.930
The Maoris, in their legend, had called it a moa.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:41.260
And Professor Owen, in his researches, had proved that it once had existed.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.590
But was it the largest that had ever lived?

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.420
There were several different species of moa, but this one was the biggest.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.010
It stands three meters tall.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.360
But is this really what it looked like when it was alive?

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:46.560
You can tell how an animal holds its head

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.310
from the junction between the skull and its neck.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:51.910
If that is underneath the skull,

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:53.730
then its neck would have been upright.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:58.590
But this mower's neck joint is at the back of the skull,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:09:59.970
so it must...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.850
have held its neck more horizontally, like this.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.310
So, was the giant mower the biggest bird that has ever existed?

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.770
Well, if it craned up its neck,

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.380
it was almost certainly the tallest.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:11.250
You might think that such a gigantic bird would have no enemies in the remote and isolated forests of New Zealand.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:26.870
There's also a Maori legend of a huge predatory bird, an eagle, that existed at the same time.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.350
And what is more, there are bones to...

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:48.730
This colossal bird was nearly twice as heavy as today's most powerful eagle.

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:53.340
Bringing down a giant mower must have been a huge task.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.020
They, too, were strong and heavy.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.490
But the eagle had powerful eyesight,

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.700
beak the size of a butcher's cleaver,

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:25.050
and razor-sharp talons as big as the claws of a tiger.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.520
The Greek for grappling hooks is half-axe.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.190
And that word gives this bird its name.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:40.520
This is Harpagornis.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.380
It was a deadly predator.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:13.850
It was the largest eagle that has ever existed, lived in the same forests as the Moas.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.200
We know that Harpagornis preyed on mowers

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:35.560
because mower skeletons have been found with holes

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.540
stabbed through their pelvic bones

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:41.580
that exactly match the grasp of the eagle's claws.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:49.630
It was probably even strong enough to cling to a mower's back with one foot

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:53.360
while it's slashed at its victim's neck with the other.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:05.570
but it looks as if this mower is going to escape for now.

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:24.310
As well as its millions of specimens of animals and plants,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:28.010
the museum also has huge and fascinating archives,

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.970
scientific journals from...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.520
all over the world letters from explorers even posters and handbills if they have anything to

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:45.740
do with natural history in the 19th century when professor owen was in charge of this museum new

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:51.980
and extraordinary things were turning up from all over the world and professor owen was very keen

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:58.470
that his museum should have the best of them he secured the archaeopteryx from germany the mowers

00:14:58.470 --> 00:14:59.190
from New Zealand

00:14:59.190 --> 00:14:59.970
but some

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.960
Sometimes, really strange things turned up on his very doorstep.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.210
And there were certainly lots of very odd creatures

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.590
being exhibited around London in Victorian times.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:16.960
This print shows an extraordinary monster

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.350
that was being displayed in Piccadilly.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.920
An American showman called Albert Koch

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:24.660
was charging a shilling a head to have a look at it.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.960
Professor Owen decided to investigate.

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:29.980
He felt...

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:36.330
Sure, that something was wrong with it, but nonetheless, he was intrigued, and he bought it.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:41.760
When he'd got it back to his museum, he was able to examine it in detail.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.970
It was certainly gigantic and big a...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.700
than anything else he had in his museum at the time.

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:08.480
Koch, the showman, had dug up the bones from a farmer's field in Missouri

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.620
and maintained that in life,

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:15.360
the animal had stood nine metres long and almost five metres tall.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:19.790
There were claims that this was a fearsome predator

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:24.240
that uses extraordinary tusks for stabbing its victims,

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:27.540
presumably by swinging its head sideways.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:29.960
Well...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.160
I'm sure Professor Owen would have had something to say about that.

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:39.750
He must have realised that these blunt, rounded ridges

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.630
on these huge molar teeth

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:46.200
would be very effective at grinding up twigs and fir cones

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.780
and rough forest vegetation,

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:53.740
but they lack the sharp blade that you need to slice through flesh.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:57.360
This is not the jaw of a carnivore.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:16:59.980
It soon became clear...

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.820
that Koch had increased the size of his monster skeleton

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:08.240
by adding extra vertebrae, ribs, and even blocks of wood.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:18.670
The Missouri Leviathan was a fraud, so Owen removed all the extra ribs.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:29.670
Then...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.590
he put the real bones back together in their true form.

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:39.880
Finally, he detached those astonishing tusks

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.860
and put them back in the correct way.

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:47.430
It seems obvious now, but in life,

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:51.690
they had pointed in much the same direction as those of a modern elephant.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:11.610
And so, here today stands not Cox's Leviathan, but Owen's Mastodon,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:19.060
a vegetarian relative of the elephant that lived 12,000 years ago in North and Central America.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:24.790
It may have decreased a bit in size, but it's still an astonishing animal.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:45.640
of the mastodon is a lot more accurate today, Mr. Professor Owen,

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.800
but it was not the only creature in this museum

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:53.280
to be the victim of misrepresentation.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:18:59.970
Poor old bird is...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:07.750
a dodo. It once lived on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean and it's almost certainly

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:14.890
the first animal species that human beings actually exterminated in historic times.

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:24.330
And so now we talk about being as dead as a dodo. But in spite of its fame,

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:29.970
this one is a fake. Its feathers come from...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:37.160
a goose, its feet were modelled on a turkey, and its beak, I suspect, is plaster.

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:45.760
The museum can be forgiven because no skin or feathers of the dodo survive.

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:53.070
Its image was influenced by pictures like this one, painted by a 17th-century Dutch artist,

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:59.890
Roland Savory, but he had never seen a living dodo and based his image on accounts by seafarers.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:05.780
I've often wondered whether dodos actually look like that.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:11.500
But unfortunately, they'd all disappeared before anyone could get a good look at them.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:13.860
Until now.

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:29.970
This funny, dumpy creature is how the bird is usually represented.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:42.200
These days have seen quite a lot of flightless birds over the years and this one doesn't quite ring true

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:54.610
Examination of the way its thighs join its pelvis has shown that in life. He'd actually stood much more upright

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:06.800
We now know that its feathers were probably a lot fluffier than in that painting.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:11.180
We also now know that it was related to the pigeon,

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:15.520
and some experts suggest that it made a pigeon-like call,

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.520
doo-doo, doo-doo, which gave the bird its name.

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:27.530
The dodo probably fell on fruit.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:29.750
There was a lot of it on the island.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:32.240
I'll try him with a bit. Come on.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:37.730
What do you make of that?

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:44.100
It's a very powerful beak.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.800
In fact, it may well have been adapted

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:50.190
for crushing shells and crustaceans

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.310
for the sake of the calcium.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:53.740
And there's a female.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:59.760
Maybe she is another reason why they had such large beaks.

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:17.820
to show off with during courtship becomes a rival male.

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:23.770
He could be another reason for having a huge beak

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:26.940
to fight with in disputes over nest sites.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:53.800
No one has ever seen a dodo egg, so no one knows how big it was.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.010
After the night, who knows?

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:24.640
Science has revealed the truth behind many a myth.

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:29.980
Discovered some creatures that are so odd as to be scarcely...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.670
But there is one story that is still remarkably persistent.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:47.010
Back in 1951, a famous Himalayan explorer and mountaineer, Eric Shipton,

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.310
came across some footprints across a high snowfield

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:54.590
that looked as if they'd been made by some kind of giant ape.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.950
Shipton's Sherpa companions had...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:13.910
no doubt about what had made them, a yeti, an abominable snowman. There is one small,

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:20.850
insignificant-looking specimen in the storage vaults down here that could perhaps explain

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:29.840
those prints. It was found in a shop in Hong Kong that sold Chinese tradition.

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:45.540
It was the molar tooth of some kind of ape-like creature,

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.020
except that it was huge.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:51.430
The museum has only got a fragment.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:52.470
This is it.

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:56.060
But here's a cast of a complete one,

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.910
and it's six times the size of one of ours.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:05.840
It was given the name Gigantopithecus, giant ape.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.770
After that discovery, one or two more teeth were discovered,

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:14.000
but nothing much until eventually a piece of the lower jaw was found.

00:25:14.810 --> 00:25:16.660
The original is now in America.

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:20.510
This is a cast, but here is the lower jaw.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:25.780
If this animal had a skull with the same proportions as those of a gorilla,

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.910
its complete skull would have been this big.

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:33.000
This was a true monster.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:28.110
we know huge ape did exist. Gigantic.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.720
He could well have stood three metres tall,

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:37.200
in which case it would have been eight times as heavy as I am.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:39.960
And if you're as heavy as that,

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:43.690
you don't spend much time climbing in trees because they won't support.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.630
So the likelihood is that his arms are quite short

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:51.180
and he walked upright.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:52.660
He was quite...

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:29.680
Top!

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:36.590
as I do. And if that head is to be well-balanced, it's better not to have a long muzzle but a rather

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:50.480
flat face. So if I were to observe Digant Pythicus and its stare, I'd find its look rather unnervingly familiar.

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:18.730
Gigantopithecus is commonly thought to have died out several hundred thousand years ago,

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.300
but sightings of the yeti continue to be reported.

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:29.100
So, is it possible that some kind of giant ape, maybe even Gigantopithecus itself,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.030
it'll survive somewhere out in those remote Himalayan mountains.

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:54.180
The Gigantopithecus tooth isn't the only intriguing specimen down here in the storerooms.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:59.130
This is a piece of dung.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:04.430
Looking at it, you might think it had dropped to the ground only yesterday.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:08.540
It was found in a cave in Patagonia.

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:15.500
And with it, a piece of skin like this,

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:20.210
covered in a very coarse, bristly hair,

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:26.840
and on the underside, mysterious white bone nodules,

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:29.860
as those kind of armour.

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:36.680
No known creature alive today has armoured hide like this.

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:42.260
If it still survived, it would be a truly extraordinary discovery.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:45.100
So, at the end of the 19th century,

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:49.280
explorers and scientists started a search for it.

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.300
In fact, the dung and the fur appeared to be recent

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.980
only because they'd been, in effect, freeze-dry.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.800
in that ancient cave.

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:06.170
The creatures themselves had died out some 10,000 years ago,

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:12.190
but explorers did find their skeletons.

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:19.630
They were giant sloths that lived not in trees, as modern ones do,

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:25.800
but on the ground, and this one had immense claws.

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:29.790
What could it have used them for?

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:08.840
These Grand Sloths probably spent most of their time on all fours,

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:13.660
but nonetheless they were perfectly capable of rearing up on their hind legs.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:20.700
When they did that, they probably stood about three metres tall,

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:23.820
which is as tall as a grizzly bear, if not taller.

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:36.530
I don't think this one is going to use its claws on me.

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:44.580
That dung made it clear that these creatures are vegetarians,

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:48.240
so they doubtless use those claws for ripping up plants.

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:54.870
But it's been discovered recently that they use them for something else as well.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:08.920
Something that seems rather surprising for animals of their great bulk.

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:29.980
They dug burrows.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:59.960
Innovations like this have been found all over Patton.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.880
and we know they were made by giant sloths

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:07.200
because scratches on the walls of the burrows exactly match their claws.

00:33:09.590 --> 00:33:14.160
Such immense burrows must have been excellent places to take refuge,

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:20.080
and the giant sloths may well have had need of them

00:33:20.420 --> 00:33:24.520
because there was a truly ferocious predator living alongside them.

