00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:19.460
No wonder the giant sloths needed burrows in which to take refuge.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:46.690
You might think that Smilodon would have caught its prey, as a lion often does,

00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:50.870
by chasing it, leaping on it at speed, and then throttling it,

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.460
suffocating it with a bite to the neck.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:34:59.970
But Smilodon...

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:20.380
talked its prey, creeping quietly across the plains until it got really close. It pounced.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:37.760
Smilodon couldn't throttle its prey with those huge teeth,

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:39.860
and they were too brittle to slash.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:42.280
They would shatter if they struck bone.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:50.420
Instead, the animal would have first used its great weight

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:52.580
to pin down its victim.

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:58.430
Then it would have used its sabres like blades

00:35:58.430 --> 00:35:59.970
to slice open the...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:08.800
off flesh of its victim's throat, but these terrifying hunters had a rather touching side

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:15.940
to their characters. Tigers today are solitary hunters, and when one gets too old to hunt

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:22.360
successfully, it dies. But skeletons of really elderly saber-tooths have been discovered,

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:29.960
which suggests that not only did Smilodon hunt in packs, but when members of the

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:35.500
