00:46:30.220 --> 00:46:33.600
So, maybe ichthyosaurs would have won the competition?

00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:35.700
After all, who knows?

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:52.890
The ichthyosaurs and other marine reptiles ruled the seas.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:59.400
150 million years ago, another group of reptiles dominated the land.

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:05.930
They lived long before big mammals, let alone human beings.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:11.480
There were hundreds, probably thousands of different kinds,

00:47:11.760 --> 00:47:14.540
and they came in all shapes and sizes.

00:47:16.060 --> 00:47:21.580
They are perhaps the most famous and dramatic of all prehistoric creatures,

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:26.620
and they were first identified and named here in Britain.

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:33.100
They were the dinosaurs.

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:42.660
Thousands of people come here every day to look at their amazing skeletons

00:47:43.080 --> 00:47:51.180
and to imagine what they must have looked like and sounded like when they were alive.

00:48:08.380 --> 00:48:13.140
It's hard to imagine a time when the world didn't know about dinosaurs,

00:48:13.800 --> 00:48:18.360
but until relatively recently, nobody knew they had ever existed,

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:21.100
let alone that they once ruled the world.

00:48:25.080 --> 00:48:29.860
The story of their discovery starts in the 1820s when a doctor...

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:33.570
named Gideon Mantell, living on the south coast of England

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:38.980
