00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:59.970
from the instantly recognizable three-horned Triceratops to the sensational Tyrannosaurus.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:10.820
dinosaurus rex. These astounding beasts have inspired and captivated not only scientists,

00:51:11.450 --> 00:51:21.260
but writers, artists, and filmmakers for almost two centuries. But it was Professor Owen here in the

00:51:21.260 --> 00:51:28.550
Natural History Museum who first identified them, and his work has been continued here ever since.

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:39.800
This is a laboratory where the museum prepares its fossils for study and for display.

00:51:47.280 --> 00:51:50.780
It's here that they painstakingly remove the excess rock

00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:54.470
to reveal the fossils in all their extraordinary detail.

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:07.060
This is the fossilized egg of a dinosaur, one of the first to be discovered.

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:11.340
And it was found close to some bones of a sauropod.

00:52:13.280 --> 00:52:14.910
Sauropods, this is the model.

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:21.480
were gigantic vegetarian dinosaurs that wandered around on four legs.

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:25.390
There are lots of different species of them, they're found all over the world,

00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:29.510
and they're the biggest land animals that have ever existed.

00:52:30.960 --> 00:52:35.220
Of course, you can't prove that it was a sauropod that laid this egg.

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:37.450
But I would like to think that it was.

00:52:38.850 --> 00:52:42.380
The weight of the sand that eventually covered it squashed it.

00:52:42.760 --> 00:52:44.980
But if we could see it when it was first laid...

00:52:45.180 --> 00:52:59.330
We would see that it's much rounder than a chicken's egg, more like that of a turtle or crocodile, and of course very much bigger.

00:53:03.548 --> 00:53:03.728
Hmm.

