has got quite a lot of growing to do over the next few years.

00:55:21.948 --> 00:55:23.188
The museum, of course.

00:55:23.676 --> 00:55:24.616
Has the skeleton?

00:55:24.988 --> 00:55:27.148
of a fully grown sauropod.

00:55:27.644 --> 00:55:28.644
of a kind.

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:34.360
and its story is one of kings and millionaires.

00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:42.190
In 1902, King Edward VII, then Prince of Wales,

00:55:42.410 --> 00:55:44.750
saw a picture of a huge sauropod...

00:55:45.030 --> 00:55:47.130
one of the biggest yet discovered,

00:55:47.540 --> 00:55:50.560
whilst visiting the Scotsman-turned-American millionaire

00:55:50.560 --> 00:55:54.120
Andrew Carnegie at his castle in Scotland.

00:55:55.810 --> 00:55:57.130
The prince immediately said,

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.290
well, I would like one of those.

00:56:00.090 --> 00:56:03.090
And in those days, what princes asked for, they got.

00:56:08.120 --> 00:56:14.120
And so, in due course, another replica turned up right here in the Natural History Museum.

00:56:14.320 --> 00:56:29.980
Here it is. There are two ways of...

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:31.540
pronouncing its scientific name.

00:56:31.890 --> 00:56:35.250
It's either Diplodocus or Diplodocus.

00:56:35.550 --> 00:56:37.940
Either way, it's a bit of a mouthful.

00:56:38.450 --> 00:56:40.490
So I'm going to use the nickname

00:56:40.490 --> 00:56:42.350
that is commonly used around here.

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:44.620
This is Dippy.

00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:47.600
And once more, although there's no way

00:56:47.600 --> 00:56:50.040
of being sure whether it was male or female,

00:56:50.690 --> 00:56:54.090
I'm going to assume that Dippy was female.

00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:59.650
What did Dippy look like?

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:00.980
when she was alive.

00:57:04.370 --> 00:57:09.230
This strangely-shaped fragment of a dinosaur called Edmontosaurus

00:57:09.230 --> 00:57:12.450
was mummified before it was fossilized.

00:57:12.820 --> 00:57:17.070
So not only the bones, but the skin was almost perfectly preserved,

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:20.140
and it was covered in small scales.

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:23.260
They didn't overlap like those of a lizard,

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:25.420
but formed a close-fitting mosaic.

00:57:26.200 --> 00:57:29.970
Maybe Dippy was like that too, but what a...

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:38.030
about her colour. My suspicion is that Dippy, like many large mammals today, such as elephants

00:57:38.030 --> 00:57:45.080
or rhinoceros, was a general all over neutral plain colour. So if we add a little bit of

00:57:45.080 --> 00:57:49.760
skin and flesh, we can get some idea of what she actually looked like.

00:58:35.210 --> 00:58:38.450
So now, after 150 million years,

00:58:38.770 --> 00:58:42.010
we've got a pretty good idea of what Dippy looked like.

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:44.380
But how did she behave?

00:58:57.880 --> 00:58:59.980
Well, animals her size and weight

00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:02.860
must have moved in a rather ponderous way.

00:59:06.220 --> 00:59:09.960
And in any case, since she was a vegetarian, as we know from her teeth,

00:59:10.630 --> 00:59:13.730
she had no need to be speedy to get her food.

00:59:17.170 --> 00:59:20.670
But it's the tiny bones in Dippy's inner ear

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:24.560
that can give us a clue as to what she sounded like.

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:29.960
These little bones are basically the same shape as that of the...

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:32.220
of the dinosaur's closest relatives, birds.

00:59:33.190 --> 00:59:37.060
The range of sounds a bird hears is related to its size.

00:59:37.500 --> 00:59:40.600
A small bird makes and hears high-pitched sounds,

00:59:41.650 --> 00:59:45.830
whereas large birds communicate with low-pitched sounds.

00:59:48.960 --> 00:59:53.740
So, huge Dippy, with her inner ear bones shaped like those of a bird,

00:59:53.940 --> 00:59:57.960
could probably hear very low-pitched frequencies of sound.

01:00:00.830 --> 01:00:02.890
And she could probably make them, too.

01:00:03.210 --> 01:00:26.230
You know that elephants today can communicate using infrasound.

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:29.630
Sound with frequencies so low they're below.

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:36.570
human hearing, and those sounds travel through the ground, sometimes for many miles, and

01:00:36.570 --> 01:00:41.760
are detected by elephants through their large, flat, sensitive feet.

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:52.260
Dippy, too, had large, flat feet, so maybe the giant dinosaurs communicated with one

01:00:52.260 --> 01:00:55.760
another in much the same way, as well as by bellowing.

01:01:00.800 --> 01:01:04.140
And those may not have been the only noises that Dippy could make.

01:01:06.820 --> 01:01:10.280
Some scientists think that because of the length of her tail

01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:11.860
and the way the joints work,

01:01:12.350 --> 01:01:14.920
she might have been able to crack it like a whip.

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:23.830
Muscular strength that enabled her to hold her tail above the ground

01:01:23.830 --> 01:01:26.850
meant that she could, if necessary, use it as a weapon.

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:35.730
Her tail would have helped to balance her long, heavy neck.

01:01:36.180 --> 01:01:38.220
But why was that so long?

01:01:40.670 --> 01:01:43.370
It used to be thought that she lived in rivers

01:01:43.370 --> 01:01:47.340
and needed her neck to break the surface in order to breathe.

01:01:47.680 --> 01:01:51.670
But that can't have been true, because if her body was submerged,

01:01:52.180 --> 01:01:55.190
the pressure of the water would have crushed her lungs.

01:01:55.190 --> 01:01:59.960
The most likely explanation...

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:06.600
seems to be that her huge neck helped her reach vast quantities of leaves. Sweeping it from side

01:02:06.600 --> 01:02:14.960
to side, she could cover a larger grazing area. She could also push her head between forest trees

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:24.460
to reach ferns and other ground vegetation. But in order to reach the highest, most succulent leaves

01:02:24.460 --> 01:02:29.920
in the forest, it seems likely that Dippy would have reared up on her hind leg.

01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:32.660
Come on, David.

